# Statement History / Recover — multi-user (browser-local)

## Invariant

History lists **only** statements saved in **this browser’s IndexedDB** on the accounting origin (`http://localhost:3001` / deployed accounting host). Recover may run only when that History has **no real bank file** and a **non-cash** browser cache still holds rows.

## What every other user gets (by design)

| Situation | Expected |
|-----------|----------|
| New merchant, empty browser | “No saved statements” + upload via Biz Intel — **no** Recover |
| Same user, other PC / Chrome profile / phone | Empty History — data does **not** sync across devices |
| Storefront port (e.g. 3005) | Never shows sandbox History (different app / origin) |
| Private window / cleared site data | Empty History — correct |
| History empty + transaction cache | Recover banner; saves `recovered_*.cache` **without** `cash_*` rows |
| Real PDF/CSV already in History | Recover **hidden and refused** (would double P&L) |
| Recover dump already present | Refuse second Recover until that row is deleted |
| IndexedDB read throws | Error banner + Retry — never silent empty list |

Cash expenses stay in the **cash** IndexedDB store. `loadAllTransactions` merges them; Recover must **strip `cash_*`** or cash doubles for every merchant.

## Do

- Gate Recover with `evaluateRecoverEligibility` (wait for `historyHydrated`)
- Filter recover payload with `filterRowsForStatementRecover`
- Surface IndexedDB read failures as an error — silent `[]` reads as “data deleted”
- Prefer re-upload of the real bank file over Recover when possible

## Do not

- Assume one merchant’s IndexedDB exists on another user’s machine
- Persist Recover beside an existing PDF/CSV
- Put cash expenses into a recovered statement file
- Treat empty History as a code bug when the browser simply has no statements
